<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xhosa people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as amaXhosa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Xhosa are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, speaking isiXhosa. In the late eighteenth and nineteenth centuries they fought a long series of frontier wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, lost much of their land and suffered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-killing movement of 1856–1857. Prominent Xhosa include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>, and the language is spoken by some eight million people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xhosa-people.jpg" alt="Xhosa people" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archives anon. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zonnebloem_College_-_Xhosa_graduates_-_Cape_Town_Cape_Colony_1860.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Xhosa are descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> clans who settled in the southeastern part of Southern Africa. These farming agropastoralists brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and goats, absorbed weaker San groups and formed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> and kingdoms, mainly in what is now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a>. Archaeological evidence suggests that Xhosa-speaking people have lived in the area since at least the 7th century. According to oral tradition the Xhosa Kingdom was founded before the 15th century by Tshawe, after whom the royal clan is named, who overthrew his brother Cirha with the help of the amaNgwevu clan of the amaMpondomise Kingdom. Khoekhoe groups such as the Inqua and the Giqwa were incorporated and became tributary to the amaTshawe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After Europeans settled the Cape in 1652 the Khoisan were gradually pushed eastwards, and by the 1700s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>'s borders had moved far enough east to create a hostile population ready to resist in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a>. This began the Cape frontier wars, some of the longest military resistance to colonialism. Large parts of the native population were confined to reserves in the easternmost Cape, though they also continued to serve as labour inside the colony. These reserves were later renamed homelands and were dismantled in 1994.</p><p>In the 19th century the Xhosa fought off and took in many peoples fleeing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulus</a> during the mfecane. The Xhosa called those they accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mfengu">amaMfengu</a>, meaning wanderers, among them the amaBhaca, amaHlubi and amaZizi. Xhosa unity and their ability to resist colonial encroachment were weakened by the famines and divisions that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-killing movement that began in 1856. Historians now see it as a millennialist response, directly to a lung disease spreading among their cattle and indirectly to the strain of losing territory and autonomy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a> are important and the basis of wealth, and the Xhosa settled on the slopes of the Amatola and Winterberg Mountains, where the Kei and Fish Rivers and rich soils suit farming and grazing. Traditional foods include beef, mutton, goat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> meal, amasi, a fermented milk, and umqombothi, a beer of fermented maize and sorghum. Marriage, umtshato, involves a sequence of customs including the discussion of the woman's clan name, isiduko, since people sharing a clan name may not marry, and lobola negotiations, which were traditionally settled in cattle and are now often paid in money. The speakers of isiXhosa, a Bantu tonal language written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet, understand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> to some degree.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xhosa people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its eastern border lay roughly along the Fish River, where settler farmers competed with <b>Xhosa</b> chiefdoms for grazing land.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>British magistrates gradually took power from <b>Xhosa</b> chiefs, and a further war in 1850-1853 tightened control.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-kaffraria">British Kaffraria</a></li><li><blockquote>Trekboer cattle farmers pushed the frontier east to the Fish River, where they met the <b>Xhosa</b>, and Afrikaans developed from Cape Dutch.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-cape-colony">Dutch Cape Colony</a></li><li><blockquote>Gcaleka's successors continued to be recognised as paramount over all <b>Xhosa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/gcaleka-xhosa-kingdom">Gcaleka Xhosa Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Graaff-Reinet, the eastern frontier district of the Dutch Cape Colony, was in turmoil over the Company's handling of conflict with the <b>Xhosa</b> and the San.</blockquote><a href="/state/graaff-reinet-republic">Graaff-Reinet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Xhosa</b> king Hintsa was shot while in British custody during the campaign.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-queen-adelaide">Province of Queen Adelaide</a></li></ul></section>
